## Summary

The change is implemented and the full suite is green (257 tests, 0 failures).

The endpoint infrastructure was already wired for a duplicate check — `POST /api/owners` runs a `CheckDuplicateOwner` step, throwing `DuplicateOwnerException` which is handled as **409 Conflict** by `DuplicateOwnerExceptionHandler`. The only issue was that the existing check required *all* fields (first name, last name, address, city, telephone) to match, so it wouldn't reject the case the spec calls for.

I changed `CheckDuplicateOwner` (`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/CheckDuplicateOwner.java`) to reject when an existing owner shares the **same last name and telephone number**:

```java
private static boolean isDuplicate(Owner existing, Owner candidate) {
    return Objects.equals(existing.getLastName(), candidate.getLastName())
            && Objects.equals(existing.getTelephone(), candidate.getTelephone());
}
```

It queries by last name (`OwnerRepository.findByLastName`), then rejects on a telephone match. I also updated the now-stale Javadoc on the exception class. The `Cp02Tests` acceptance tests (core reject on same last name + telephone → 409; allow same last name with different telephone → 2xx) both pass, as does the byte-identical `Cp01` case (identical owners still share last name + telephone).

